    "obs_and_metrics": [],
    "innovation": "This paper demonstrates how decadal North Atlantic warming/cooling cycles are coupled to European shelf sea-level variability through the forcing of synchronized along-slope winds, which drive cross-shelf exchange and modulate the rate of coastal sea-level rise.",
    "kg_extraction_triplets": [
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Atlantic Meridional Overturning Circulation",
            "source_entity_type": "Ocean_Circulation",
            "relation": "OBSERVED_IN_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "North Atlantic",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "Recent observational evidence18 shows that the strength of the Atlantic Meridional Overturning Circulation or AMOC\u00b9\u2079, i.e. the flow of warm surface waters polewards and the return of cold deep waters equatorwards, is indeed the major factor regulating the warm and cold decades of the North Atlantic as has long been hypothesized 20.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "subpolar gyre",
            "source_entity_type": "Ocean_Circulation",
            "relation": "OBSERVED_IN_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "North Atlantic",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "In the North Atlantic, numerous studies have shown the marked increase in sea level over the Subpolar North Atlantic (SPNA), a key climatic region where the maximum node of the Atlantic multidecadal variability is found23\u201325, and associated spin-down of the gyre circulation to be a response to a decrease in wind stress curl over the region during mid-1990s26,27.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Atlantic Meridional Overturning Circulation",
            "source_entity_type": "Ocean_Circulation",
            "relation": "OBSERVED_IN_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "Labrador Sea",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "However, a recent study reports that this SPNA warming led to a reduction of the deep-water formation rate in the Labrador Sea, which led to an anomalously weak poleward heat transport and thereby",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Gulf Stream",
            "source_entity_type": "Ocean_Circulation",
            "relation": "OBSERVED_IN_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "North Atlantic",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "Changes in dynamic and steric sea level from altimetry show a significant upward trend in the eastern North Atlantic, the subpolar gyre and interior Nordic Seas during the WP (Fig. 1a,b). In contrast, these trends are reversed in the mid-latitudes and over the Gulf Stream (GS) region.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "subpolar gyre",
            "source_entity_type": "Ocean_Circulation",
            "relation": "OBSERVED_IN_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "Nordic Seas",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "This contraction-expansion of the subpolar gyre is important as it regulates the inflow of warmer and saline subtropical waters into the northeast Atlantic, subpolar gyre and the Nordic Seas26-28,48.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": false,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "North Atlantic Oscillation",
            "source_entity_type": "Teleconnection",
            "relation": "INFLUENCES_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "North Atlantic",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "The anomalously low sea level during the past few winters (2014\u20132016) can be attributed to the exceptionally strong North Atlantic Oscillation49 and hence a return to the conditions seen during the early 1990s.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "North Atlantic Current",
            "source_entity_type": "Ocean_Circulation",
            "relation": "OBSERVED_IN_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "North Atlantic",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "During the CP, however, the MKE experiences a negative trend in the northern North Atlantic, and a positive trend that extends more to the east in a zonal manner east of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge along the path of the North Atlantic Current (NAC), signifying an overall southeast shift of the NAC and subpolar gyre (Fig. 2d).",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "North Atlantic Oscillation",
            "source_entity_type": "Teleconnection",
            "relation": "INFLUENCES_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "European shelves",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "During this recent period, European shelf and coastal sea levels increased rapidly (e.g. Fig. 3b) as a response to the strong westerly winds associated with the North Atlantic Oscillation, despite an anomalously cold North Atlantic Ocean.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "DUACS DT2014",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "PRODUCES_VARIABLE",
            "target_entity_instance": "absolute dynamic topography",
            "target_entity_type": "Variable",
            "paragraph": "The DUACS DT2014 multi-mission satellite altimetry68 is here utilized to study the sea-level trends and variability in the North Atlantic and on the European shelves. We use the 1/4\u00b0 degree monthly mean absolute dynamic topography (the sum of sea-level anomalies and the MDT_CNES/CLS 2013 ocean mean dynamic topography) averaged from daily data between January 1993 and December 2016.",
            "ontology_conformed": false,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "EN4v2",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "PRODUCES_VARIABLE",
            "target_entity_instance": "temperature",
            "target_entity_type": "Variable",
            "paragraph": "The temperature and salinity data are based on the monthly mean objectively analyzed hydrographic data from the UK Met Office, EN4v269, with the bias taken into account using the correction by Gouretski and Resghetti (2010)70.",
            "ontology_conformed": false,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "EN4v2",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "PRODUCES_VARIABLE",
            "target_entity_instance": "salinity",
            "target_entity_type": "Variable",
            "paragraph": "The temperature and salinity data are based on the monthly mean objectively analyzed hydrographic data from the UK Met Office, EN4v269, with the bias taken into account using the correction by Gouretski and Resghetti (2010)70.",
            "ontology_conformed": false,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "EN4v2",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "PRODUCES_VARIABLE",
            "target_entity_instance": "steric height",
            "target_entity_type": "Variable",
            "paragraph": "We use the 1993-2016 period to calculate in-situ density using the non-linear equation of state71, and later integrate the density departures from the time-mean (1993-2016) over the upper 1000 m of the water column to derive the steric height:",
            "ontology_conformed": false,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": false,
            "eval": false
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "NCEP/NCAR reanalysis",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "PRODUCES_VARIABLE",
            "target_entity_instance": "wind stress",
            "target_entity_type": "Variable",
            "paragraph": "For the atmospheric circulation analysis, we use the NCEP/NCAR reanalysis74 monthly zonal and meridional winds and wind stress spanning the 1993\u20132016 period.",
            "ontology_conformed": false,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "NCEP/NCAR reanalysis",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "PRODUCES_VARIABLE",
            "target_entity_instance": "wind stress curl",
            "target_entity_type": "Variable",
            "paragraph": "For the atmospheric circulation analysis, we use the NCEP/NCAR reanalysis74 monthly zonal and meridional winds and wind stress spanning the 1993\u20132016 period. The horizontal resolution of these data is 2.5\u00b0 \u00d7 2.5\u00b0. The data have been deseasonalized by removing the mean seasonal cycle prior to the analysis. The wind-stress data are further used to calculate the wind stress curl in the North Atlantic region.",
            "ontology_conformed": false,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": false,
            "eval": false
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "PSMSL",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "PRODUCES_VARIABLE",
            "target_entity_instance": "sea-level",
            "target_entity_type": "Variable",
            "paragraph": "To obtain an estimate of regional coastal sea-level changes, we use tide-gauge data from the Permanent Service for Mean Sea Level or PSMSL76.",
            "ontology_conformed": false,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        }
